    {
      "text": "obviously it's not the LLM which failed, right? It's the way how we split the work, right? Because we tried mimicking the analyst behavior, and we did it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 277,
      "why": "The thesis of the talk: the failure was architectural decomposition, not model capability."
    },
    {
      "text": "at each level if you see, it has actually derived the right fact, but then there is no single agent which is owning which understands the end-to-end picture basically.",
      "timestamp_sec": 243,
      "why": "Names the precise failure mode — locally correct, globally incoherent output."
    },
    {
      "text": "signals like things like your sales drop is a simple information which you can use statistical methods to actually go and fetch this information. You don't need a language model actually, right? To fetch this information.",
      "timestamp_sec": 277,
      "why": "States the boundary between deterministic and agentic work concretely."
    },
    {
      "text": "as your multi agents, there is a lot of context hand off which is happening, and context is actually getting lost at each of these hand offs",
      "timestamp_sec": 277,
      "why": "The core cost of multi-agent decomposition they identify."
    },
    {
      "text": "the last big piece is there is no shared understanding of the business domain knowledge for all these agents. All these agents don't understand metrics",
      "timestamp_sec": 315,
      "why": "Sets up the knowledge-graph half of the solution."
    },
    {
      "text": "We didn't do any of that. We like all of us, we went back to Cloud Code. So, we opened a very plain empty directory. I ran Cloud Code. Then give it just bash and the database.",
      "timestamp_sec": 386,
      "why": "Unusual methodology: derive architecture by observing a general coding agent instead of redesigning topology."
    },
    {
      "text": "This is something we don't want an agent to do. This is a completely deterministic workflow. So, we separated it out from the agentic system.",
      "timestamp_sec": 428,
      "why": "The clearest statement of their deterministic/agentic split."
    },
    {
      "text": "The moment a signal comes to the queue, the agent wakes up. So, the agent's job is to investigate, not to identify.",
      "timestamp_sec": 469,
      "why": "A memorable one-line reframing of the agent's scope."
    },
    {
      "text": "What we removed is, do we need distributed reasoning? We didn't but the judgment to be distributed between agents. That we wanted to consolidate to a single agent.",
      "timestamp_sec": 510,
      "why": "Distinguishes parallelism (kept) from distributed reasoning (removed)."
    },
    {
      "text": "That you can still delegate to a sub-agent. You can get back the uh results back, not the reasoning or the judgment.",
      "timestamp_sec": 510,
      "why": "Defines exactly what sub-agents are allowed to return."
    },
    {
      "text": "the agent was looking at data, looking at tables, then trying to infer the relationship. That which was not scalable. And it often produce relationship which which is not actually exist in the data.",
      "timestamp_sec": 551,
      "why": "Motivates encoding domain structure explicitly rather than letting the model infer it."
    },
    {
      "text": "the knowledge graph is not just something the agent looks up for data. It is a control plane for the agent.",
      "timestamp_sec": 632,
      "why": "The talk's signature claim about knowledge graphs."
    },
    {
      "text": "the knowledge graphs dictates what the agent can look into, what path it can take, what investigation hypothesis uh it can evaluate",
      "timestamp_sec": 632,
      "why": "Spells out what 'control plane' means operationally."
    },
    {
      "text": "every edge is a hypothesis. So, the agent can go and evaluate that hypothe- hypothesis. Uh it doesn't go outside of this.",
      "timestamp_sec": 708,
      "why": "Compact statement of how the graph bounds the agent's search."
    },
    {
      "text": "after like 50 plus turns, a whole lot of tokens, it's able to produce something an analyst was able to produce maybe in three or four weeks in like maybe 20 30 minutes",
      "timestamp_sec": 790,
      "why": "The only quantified outcome claim in the talk, including its token cost."
    },
    {
      "text": "First thing is I think we should not be introducing human constraints or design constraints into architecture.",
      "timestamp_sec": 790,
      "why": "First of four takeaways and the general lesson behind killing the pipeline."
    },
    {
      "text": "any complex workflows will have deterministic parts and agentic parts. Don't let agents actually run the deterministic part, right?",
      "timestamp_sec": 831,
      "why": "Portable design rule stated as an imperative."
    },
    {
      "text": "I think graph cannot be treated just as a lookup layer. I think graph has to be treated as a control plane which the agent uses to navigate and takes the next decisions basically.",
      "timestamp_sec": 831,
      "why": "The takeaway they flag as most important."
    }
